::ALG2_U05_L05_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x+1 and g(x)=2x, what is (f+g)(x)? {
= 3x+1#Correct. Add x+1 and 2x.
~ 2x<sup>2</sup>+1#This multiplies.
~ 3x#This omits 1.
~ 2x+1#This is f(g(x)).
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x-3 and g(x)=4x, what is (f-g)(x)? {
~ 5x-3#This adds instead of subtracting.
= -3x-3#Correct. Subtract: x-3-4x=-3x-3.
~ 3x+3#This subtracts in the wrong order.
~ 4x-3#This is f(g(x)).
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x and g(x)=x+2, what is (fg)(x)? {
~ 2x+2#This adds the function rules instead of multiplying them.
~ x+2#This is just g(x).
= x<sup>2</sup>+2x#Correct. Multiply x(x+2).
~ x<sup>2</sup>+2#This misses the product term.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x+5 and g(x)=x-1, what is f(g(x))? {
~ x+6#This uses x+1.
~ x<sup>2</sup>+4x-5#This multiplies the functions.
~ 2x+4#This adds the functions.
= x+4#Correct. Substitute x-1 into x+5.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=2x+1 and g(x)=x<sup>2</sup>, what is f(g(x))? {
= 2x<sup>2</sup>+1#Correct. Substitute x<sup>2</sup> into f.
~ (2x+1)<sup>2</sup>#This is g(f(x)).
~ 2x+1+x<sup>2</sup>#This adds functions.
~ 2x<sup>3</sup>+x<sup>2</sup>#This multiplies.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x<sup>2</sup> and g(x)=x+3, what is g(f(x))? {
~ (x+3)<sup>2</sup>#This is f(g(x)).
= x<sup>2</sup>+3#Correct. Substitute x<sup>2</sup> into g.
~ x<sup>2</sup>(x+3)#This multiplies.
~ 2x+3#This is not the composition.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=3x and g(x)=x-2, what is g(f(4))? {
~ 6#This applies g to 4 first.
~ 12#This stops after f(4).
= 10#Correct. f(4)=12, then g(12)=10.
~ 18#This adds instead of subtracting.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x+4 and g(x)=2x, what is f(g(5))? {
~ 18#This applies f first, then g.
~ 10#This stops after g(5).
~ 9#This applies f to 5 only.
= 14#Correct. g(5)=10 and f(10)=14.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>For (f/g)(x) with g(x)=x-6, what value is excluded? {
= x=6#Correct. The denominator is zero when x=6.
~ x=-6#This has the wrong sign.
~ x=0#g(0)=-6, not zero.
~ No values#A denominator zero must be excluded.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>For (f/g)(x) with g(x)=x+3, what value is excluded? {
~ x=3#This uses the opposite sign; x+3 equals zero when x=-3.
= x=-3#Correct. The denominator is zero when x=-3.
~ x=0#g(0)=3, so the denominator is not zero at x=0.
~ All real numbers#Only x=-3 is excluded.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which notation means composition? {
~ f(x)g(x)#This means multiplication.
~ f(x)+g(x)#This means addition.
= f(g(x))#Correct. This means apply g first, then f.
~ f(x)-g(x)#This means subtraction.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x<sup>2</sup> and g(x)=2x, what is f(g(3))? {
~ 18#This multiplies 6 by 3.
~ 12#This adds steps incorrectly.
~ 9#This applies f to 3 only.
= 36#Correct. g(3)=6 and f(6)=36.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x+7 and g(x)=x/2, what is f(g(10))? {
= 12#Correct. g(10)=5 and f(5)=12.
~ 8.5#This applies f first and then divides.
~ 5#This stops after g(10).
~ 17#This applies f to 10 only.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x-1 and g(x)=x+1, what is f(g(x))? {
~ x+2#This adds functions.
= x#Correct. Substitute x+1 into x-1.
~ x<sup>2</sup>-1#This multiplies functions.
~ x-2#This uses the wrong order.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=2x and g(x)=x+8, what is (f+g)(4)? {
~ 16#This omits part of the sum.
~ 12#This is f(4)+g(0) style error.
= 20#Correct. (f+g)(x)=3x+8, so at x=4 the value is 20.
~ 40#This multiplies the outputs.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x+2 and g(x)=x-2, what is (fg)(3)? {
~ 6#This adds the outputs.
~ 1#This is g(3) only.
~ 25#This squares f(3).
= 5#Correct. f(3)=5 and g(3)=1, so product is 5.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is the domain issue in dividing functions? {
= The denominator function cannot equal zero.#Correct. Division by zero is undefined.
~ The numerator cannot equal zero.#A zero numerator is allowed.
~ The input must be positive only.#That depends on the functions involved.
~ There is never a restriction.#Denominator zeros create restrictions.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x<sup>2</sup> and g(x)=x-4, what is f(g(x))? {
~ x<sup>2</sup>-4#This subtracts after squaring.
= (x-4)<sup>2</sup>#Correct. Substitute x-4 into f.
~ x<sup>2</sup>(x-4)#This multiplies.
~ 2x-4#This is not the composition.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x+3 and g(x)=x<sup>2</sup>, what is g(f(x))? {
~ x<sup>2</sup>+3#This is f(g(x)).
~ x<sup>2</sup>+3x#This multiplies incorrectly.
= (x+3)<sup>2</sup>#Correct. Substitute x+3 into g.
~ 2x+3#This is not composition.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=5x and g(x)=x/5, what is f(g(x))? {
~ 25x#This multiplies functions.
~ x/25#This composes in the wrong way.
~ 10x#This adds outputs.
= x#Correct. Substitute x/5 into 5x.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x+1 and g(x)=x-1, what is g(f(x))? {
= x#Correct. Substitute x+1 into x-1.
~ x+2#This adds functions.
~ x-2#This uses the wrong order.
~ x<sup>2</sup>-1#This multiplies functions.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is f(g(x)) if f(x)=3x+2 and g(x)=x-5? {
~ 3x-3#This does not distribute 3 over x-5.
= 3x-13#Correct. Substitute x-5 into 3x+2.
~ 3x+7#This uses x+5.
~ 3x<sup>2</sup>-13x-10#This multiplies functions.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is g(f(x)) if f(x)=3x+2 and g(x)=x-5? {
~ 3x-13#This is f(g(x)).
~ 3x+7#This adds 5 instead of subtracting.
= 3x-3#Correct. Substitute 3x+2 into x-5.
~ 3x<sup>2</sup>-13x-10#This multiplies functions.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If a context combines revenue and cost using R(x)-C(x), what does the result represent? {
~ Total cost#That is C(x).
~ Total revenue#That is R(x).
~ Input quantity#The input is x, not the difference.
= Profit#Correct. Revenue minus cost is profit.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L05_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(g(x)) and g(f(x)) give different rules, what does that show? {
= Composition order matters.#Correct. Function composition is generally not commutative.
~ One function is invalid.#Both may be valid.
~ The functions are equal.#Different rules show they are not equal.
~ Composition is multiplication.#Composition is substitution.
}

